  '/trade/desk':
    'Walked and built 2026-09-18 against Trade Desk.dc.html at page rev 2026-09-18.1, on local DEV (:5173). Built from nothing under the Owner\u2019s ruling of that day (option A): the three lanes the design draws \u2014 Decide / Execute / Settle \u2014 the four-cell strip above them, Rules in force below, and the hedge menu in the strip. **The hedge menu is a move, not a new capability.** Suspend, resume and emergency flatten were on the Daemon page under System, moved to Strategy \u203a Instances when the Owner ruled they are trading decisions rather than daemon telemetry, and land here because this is the trader\u2019s home; the same three endpoints, the same consumer, the same semantics, and the card was removed from Instances rather than left to exist twice. The acknowledgement on flatten is the only thing added, and it is not ceremony: suspend and resume change what the daemon will do next, flatten asks it to act in the market now. D10 continues to govern and is held where it has always been held \u2014 the spine and the daemon overlays, never the presence of a button. Measured on DEV before the lanes were built, because what fills them decides what they can say: two pending Research order intents (advisory, `d10: BLOCKED` in their own envelope), zero intended plans, zero open orders in IB, 478 executions with 201 carrying no instance, and 13 short legs of which 7 carry no spot and the nearest expiry is 28 days out. So the Decide lane is real today, Execute is legitimately empty and Settle is grouped rather than listed \u2014 201 orphan rows drawn as 201 cards would bury the two that arrived this morning, and Orders & Fills is where they are read one at a time. An empty lane prints what it means that it is empty; a blank panel would read as broken. Deliberate calls: a short leg with no spot is counted and named, never folded into the safe ones (the same rule the status bar and Positions use); `Needs you` counts what is genuinely on the Owner, so a settled-and-claimed fill and a merely unpriced leg are left out of it rather than inflating a call to act. Two of the four strip cells are marked rather than invented. Session reads no market clock: nothing on this side reports a session, a close time or an exchange calendar, and the browser\u2019s own clock is not a reading (\u00a711.3.1). `Pressure now \u2192 if all fill` takes the first half from the broker\u2019s own excess liquidity per account and marks the second: what an intent would consume needs its collateral, and `strategy_plan` stores the target, the stop and the limit, never the cash. The design\u2019s Proposal \u2192 Opportunity flow is wired \u2014 Create opportunity opens the New opportunity sheet on Rules already filled in, through the mapper Strategy \u203a Opportunity already used, now shared so a proposal turned into a rule from either side is the same write. `useRulesChain` moved to `hooks/` in the same pass, with `ChainData`, because Desk and Rules now read the same five rows and one query key is what keeps them from disagreeing about which allocation is active. Not built: the design\u2019s per-item Dismiss and Acknowledge, which need a store for \u201cI have seen this\u201d that no table on this side has \u2014 an item that reappeared tomorrow after being dismissed would be worse than one that never offered the button. Owner to look before aligned.'
